---
title: From writing code to directing AI
date: 2026-07-13
summary: My last role integrating AI changed how I see the job. The future is less about writing every line and more about directing AI to solve real business problems.
draft: false
---

For most of my career the job was clear. Take a problem, write the code, ship it. Nine years of that, mostly in Python, Django, and React. I was good at it, and I still do it every day.

Then my last role put me deep into integrating AI, and it changed how I see the work.

## The realization

Once I was building real features on large language models, the kind a team depends on, I saw how much these tools can actually do. A lot of what I used to write by hand, a model can now handle, if you set it up well. The valuable skill started shifting from typing every line to deciding what the AI should do, giving it the right context, and checking that the output holds up.

That is a different job. Less "write the solution," more "direct the solution."

## The fundamentals still decide who wins

This is not throwing away the last nine years. The opposite. Directing AI well takes exactly what old-school engineering teaches: designing a clean data model, knowing when an answer is wrong, wiring up retrieval so a model answers from real data with `embeddings` and `RAG`, and keeping the whole thing reliable in production on `AWS`. Without those, AI output is just a confident guess.

The people who get the most out of AI are not the ones who skipped the craft. They are the ones who learned it and now point it at the model.

## Where I am headed

So I am going all in. My aim is to be an AI engineer who orchestrates these tools to solve business problems, not someone shipping the same outdated solution a slower way. The tools finally caught up to the ambition, and I would rather build with where the field is going than defend where it has been.
